    "narrative": "The Austin Waves offense faces a high-variance slate today, with **Andy Pages** emerging as our clear priority start at a 71.21 efficiency score. Facing Kochanowicz and his inflated 5.12 xERA, **Andy Pages** benefits from the highest SP skill advantage of the day (+19%), providing a stable foundation in a game where offensive ceiling is prioritized. Similarly, **Gunnar Henderson** earns a strong 64.41 projection; while he faces a competent arm in Fisher, the combination of his #2 spot in the order and Fisher\u2019s slow delivery\u2014which facilitates a +2% bump for stolen base potential\u2014makes **Gunnar Henderson** an elite play for our 5x5 constraints. **Sam Antonacci** is our final high-leverage start, leveraging a prime leadoff spot and a strong 0.379 xwOBA to punish Painter, who lacks the dominance to suppress this lineup.\n\nWe remain cautious regarding the matchup against E-Rod, which forces a split-decision on our middle infield. While **Nasim Nunez** (60.43) is locked in for his speed utility, **Curtis Mead** (53.74) is marginally higher on the bench-bubble, though he keeps his spot due to the favorable platoon-adjusted upside. The most difficult personnel decision was benching **Nolan Schanuel** (43.95). Despite a respectable profile, **Nolan Schanuel** was squeezed out by the sheer volume of high-scoring, speed-additive players required to keep us competitive in the SB category. He fell just short of the efficiency required to usurp **Josh Jung** (55.75) or **Dominic Smith** (42.18), with the latter providing platoon advantages that ultimately edged out the bench depth. \n\nInvestors should monitor the risk profile of **Jackson Merrill** (53.68) and **Miguel Vargas** (58.62), as both are neutralized slightly by the presence of elite catchers behind the plate. With opposing backstops sporting sub-1.90s pop times, the aggressive baserunning required to keep our SB pace high carries elevated risk of caught-stealings. Additionally, **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (57.69) remains a start, but his score is suppressed by Bradish\u2019s 3.62 xERA and a negative platoon shift; we are banking on his pure contact profile to maintain efficiency despite the matchup friction. Stay alert for any late lineup shifts, as a change in the bottom-of-the-order slots could trigger further re-evaluations against our current benchmarks.",
